#import "../../../proofs.typ": speech, footnote

== 2. Of God And Of The Holy Trinity

#speech[#smallcaps[Paragraph 1]: The Lord our God is but one only living and true God;#footnote[1 Corinthians 8:4, 6; Deuteronomy 6:4] whose subsistence is in and of Himself,#footnote[Jeremiah 10:10; Isaiah 48:12] infinite in being and perfection; whose essence cannot be comprehended by any but Himself;#footnote[Exodus 3:14] a most pure spirit,#footnote[John 4:24] invisible, without body, parts, or passions, who only hath immortality, dwelling in the light which no man can approach unto;#footnote[1 Timothy 1:17; Deuteronomy 4:15–16] who is immutable,#footnote[Malachi 3:6] immense,#footnote[1 Kings 8:27; Jeremiah 23:23] eternal,#footnote[Psalm 90:2] incomprehensible, almighty,#footnote[Genesis 17:1] every way infinite, most holy,#footnote[Isaiah 6:3] most wise, most free, most absolute; working all things according to the counsel of His own immutable and most righteous will,#footnote[Psalm 115:3; Isaiah 46:10] for His own glory;#footnote[Proverbs 16:4; Romans 11:36] most loving, gracious, merciful, long-suffering, abundant in goodness and truth, forgiving iniquity, transgression, and sin; the rewarder of them that diligently seek Him,#footnote[Exodus 34:6–7; Hebrews 11:6] and withal most just and terrible in His judgments,#footnote[Nehemiah 9:32–33] hating all sin,#footnote[Psalm 5:5–6] and who will by no means clear the guilty.#footnote[Exodus 34:7; Nahum 1:2–3]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Paragraph 2]: God, having all life,#footnote[John 5:26] glory,#footnote[Psalm 148:13] goodness,#footnote[Psalm 119:68] blessedness, in and of Himself, is alone in and unto Himself all-sufficient, not standing in need of any creature which He hath made, nor deriving any glory from them,#footnote[Job 22:2–3] but only manifesting His own glory in, by, unto, and upon them; He is the alone fountain of all being, of whom, through whom, and to whom are all things,#footnote[Romans 11:34–36] and He hath most sovereign dominion over all creatures, to do by them, for them, or upon them, whatsoever Himself pleaseth;#footnote[Daniel 4:25, 34–35] in His sight all things are open and manifest,#footnote[Hebrews 4:13] His knowledge is infinite, infallible, and independent upon the creature, so as nothing is to Him contingent or uncertain;#footnote[Ezekiel 11:5; Acts 15:18] He is most holy in all His counsels, in all His works,#footnote[Psalm 145:17] and in all His commands; to Him is due from angels and men, whatsoever worship,#footnote[Revelation 5:12–14] service, or obedience, as creatures they owe unto the Creator, and whatever He is further pleased to require of them.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Paragraph 3]: In this divine and infinite Being there are three subsistences, the Father, the Word (or Son), and Holy Spirit,#footnote[1 John 5:7; Matthew 28:19; 2 Corinthians 13:14] of one substance, power, and eternity, each having the whole divine essence, yet the essence undivided:#footnote[Exodus 3:14; John 14:11; 1 Corinthians 8:6] the Father is of none, neither begotten nor proceeding; the Son is eternally begotten of the Father;#footnote[John 1:14, 18] the Holy Spirit proceeding from the Father and the Son;#footnote[John 15:26; Galatians 4:6] all infinite, without beginning, therefore but one God, who is not to be divided in nature and being, but distinguished by several peculiar relative properties and personal relations; which doctrine of the Trinity is the foundation of all our communion with God, and comfortable dependence on Him.]
